-- statement:
--   Exact primorial certificate for the primes up to $3499$:
--   $$\prod_{p \le 3499,\; p \text{ prime}} p = N \quad\text{and}\quad \prod_{p \le 3499,\; p \text{ prime}} (p-1) = D,$$
--   with $N$ and $D$ the two explicit integers stated in the formal statement (a 1481-digit primorial and its shifted product). This is a pure finite data certificate: it anchors the Rosser–Schoenfeld product-bound legs above $3000$ the same way the primorial certificates at $691$, $1049$, $1499$, $1999$, $2477$ and $2999$ anchor the legs below, so that each range leg only has to telescope the Euler product over its own primes instead of re-verifying the primorial from scratch.
-- source:
--   Pure finite computation certificate (companion to TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_691, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1049, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1499, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1999, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_2477 and TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_2999): the exact values of the primorial $\prod_{p\le 3499} p$ and of $\prod_{p\le 3499}(p-1)$, to serve as the verified anchor for the range legs of Rosser–Schoenfeld Theorem 23 (4.10) above $3000$.

import Mathlib.NumberTheory.PrimeCounting

namespace TaoFivePrimes
theorem primorial_certificate_3499 :
    ∏ p ∈ Nat.primesLE 3499, (p : ℕ) =  ∧
    ∏ p ∈ Nat.primesLE 3499, ((p : ℕ) - 1) =  := by sorry
